    "q-118632": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118632",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167682/assets/99184bd48b6c36ddf3e2a818f428640deec3888879336a2207ced00b9830e808.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best upper bound for the length of $\\overset{―}{BD},$ obtained using the triangle inequality theorem only?\n\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BAD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & <BA+AD \\\\ BD & <4\\,\\text{cm}+5\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BD & <9\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BCD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & <BC+CD \\\\ BD & <2\\,\\text{cm}+7\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BD & <9\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince in both cases $BD<9\\,\\text{cm}$  must be satisfied, we conclude that \n\n$$\nBD<9\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
