    "q-76098": {
      "dom_id": "step-q76098",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167682/assets/06d5347f5827a4177b93fb633703ad0992cb85d938a9d88b1e2d55aa65b7beb3.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the best upper bound for the length of $\\overset{―}{BC},$ obtained using the triangle inequality theorem only?\n\n(*Note: Figure not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "The triangle inequality theorem states that the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BAC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <BA+AC \\\\ BC & <8\\,\\text{cm}+3\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BC & <11\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Applying the triangle inequality theorem to $△BDC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <BD+DC \\\\ BC & <4\\,\\text{cm}+9\\,\\text{cm} \\\\ BC & <13\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $BC<11\\,\\text{cm}$ and $BC<13\\,\\text{cm}$ must be satisfied, we conclude that \n\n$$\nBC<11\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
